  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Frost Advisories again tonight before a warming \ntrend kicks off the new workweek. \n\nHigh pressure will continue to drift south and west toward the \ncentral Appalachians tonight yielding less of a marine influence due \nto westerly low level flow. This will set us up for one more good \nradiational cooling night with a classic ridge-valley split. Given \nthe westerly low level flow and sharp surface inversion expect \nwarmer temperatures over the mountains/ridges compared to the valley \nfloors and colder rural pockets along/west of I-95. For that reason, \nFrost Advisories have been issued for portions of northern/central \nMaryland and northern/central Virginia where the growing season is \ncurrently active. The growing season has since ended in locations \nwest of where the Advisories have been issued (i.e along and west of \nthe Blue Ridge). \n\nSome subtle changes to temperatures have been noted in the near term \nbased upon current hi-res guidance. Ended up with a blend of MOS, \nNBM 10th percentile, ConShort, and the regional Canadian to \nhighlight the colder temperatures for areas points east of the \nmountains given the lack of a marine influence and SKC conditions as \nthe high drifts overhead. Expect lows in the low to mid 30s in the \nFrost Advisory area as well as in the colder valleys west of the \nBlue Ridge. Mountain ridgetop locations and areas near the bay will \nsee lows in the low to mid 40s. \n\nWarming temperatures kick off the new workweek given downsloping low \nlevel westerly flow. Expect high temperatures to rise above normal \nMonday and Tuesday afternoon with highs in the upper 60s and low 70s \n(low to mid 60s mountains). Minimal cloud cover and light winds out \nof the west and northwest will persist allowing a warmer airmass to \nmix to the surface. Expect seasonable temperatures to continue into \nWednesday with even warmer weather arriving Thursday into late week. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions persist with increasing\ntemperatures through the end of the week.\n\nUpper-level ridging builds over the Mid-Atlantic through the end of \nthe week, resulting in continued high pressure and dry, stable \nconditions across the region. After a slight cool down Wednesday\nbehind the backdoor front, temperatures should gradually warm \nsome, with afternoon highs reaching the mid-70s by Friday with \novernight lows in the 50s. As the front stalls and starts \nlifting back north, some extra clouds may form in the warm \nadvection pattern Wednesday into early Thursday. Mostly sunny\nskies will return Thursday into Friday.\n\nA few models point to this upper-level ridging breaking down \ninto zonal flow overhead by the start of the weekend, although \nsurface high pressure could linger for a bit longer. Overall, \nthere is still no significant precipitation in the forecast \nthrough the end of the week, although will continue to monitor a\npotential cold front off to the west that could approach the \nregion by the end of next weekend.",
